# Volume 2 — Named Object and Identity Formation

**Status:** normative computational design theory  
**Sensory identity, realism, and similarity:** `NOT TESTED`

## Construct

Depth begins with an object worth deepening. A perfume named only “floral,”
“woody,” “sexy,” or “luxurious” is not sufficiently constrained for target-led
depth work. The design must identify what the wearer is supposed to encounter:
a white champi under glass, carved sandalwood warmed by skin, a wet mineral
chamber, a violet cosmetic powder, a tobacco leaf in a cedar box, an abstract
aldehydic fabric, or an intentional hybrid. The object may be realistic or
abstract, but it must have an invariant identity, an emotional tone, a family
hierarchy, and forbidden drift.

An odor object is **configural** when the whole is experienced as a distinct
quality rather than merely a list of components. Configural processing is not
the enemy of complexity. It is often the condition that lets a perfume feel
complete. Human observers have difficulty identifying more than a few
component odors in mixtures, including mixtures whose component objects are
themselves chemically complex. Therefore, a sophisticated formula should not
be judged by how many rows can be named during one sniff. It should be judged
by whether the whole retains identity while allowing meaningful internal
attributes, contrasts, and changes to be perceived.

**Recognizers** are cues whose presence supports the target identity. They can
be chemicals, naturals, accord-level patterns, ratios, textures, or temporal
events. A recognizer is not necessarily unique to one object. Linalool-like,
indolic, iononic, salicylate, smoky, leathery, or lactonic cues occur in many
contexts. Identity comes from their organized pattern, relative salience, and
relationship to other cues. The design must distinguish a broad family
recognizer from an exact target recognizer. “White floral present” is weaker
than “white champi remains distinguishable from muguet, jasmine, tuberose, and
orange-blossom drift.”

**Identity hierarchy** answers which object leads, which co-lead, which
support, and what must never invert. A bouquet can be one organism without
making every flower equal. A hybrid can contain floral, wood, leather, and
amber while remaining flower-led. If the persistent base becomes the only
legible object after thirty minutes, the formula may have performance but has
not necessarily preserved the named identity.

**Realism** and **recognizability** are separate. A stylized rose may be
immediately recognizable but botanically incomplete. A natural extract may be
botanically sourced yet fail to recreate the living flower in a perfume
matrix. Realism can refer to anatomy, environmental context, developmental
stage, humidity, decay, or temporal behavior. The target must state which kind
is desired. “Photorealistic living flower at dusk” imposes different
requirements from “crystalline memory of a flower.”

**Abstraction** is not lack of identity. An abstract perfume can define an
object through stable relations: bright discontinuity over waxy body, cold
mineral seam through warm wood, powder suspended over skin. The abstraction
fails when any generic material substitution produces the same description or
when the design has no observable invariant.

## Mechanisms

Identity can be built through an anchor pattern, a distinctive ratio, a
configural blend, a temporal signature, or a combination. An **anchor** is the
minimum pattern whose removal predictably damages the named object. It should
not be confused with the largest dose. A trace can be identity-critical while
a large structural material remains generic.

**Convergent recognition** occurs when several partially diagnostic cues point
to the same object. This can make identity robust to adaptation or variation.
**Competitive recognition** occurs when one cue activates a neighboring
category and pulls the object away. **Hierarchical recognition** lets one
subject lead while co-leads enrich its body. **Temporal recognition** allows
different cues to carry the same identity at different times; the opening and
drydown need not smell identical, but the later state must be interpretable as
the same object transformed.

Small ratio changes can alter a blending mixture's typicality even when the
changed component is difficult to identify separately. That finding requires
the formulation method to treat ratios as identity parameters, not merely
volume balance. It also means that adding another recognizer is not always the
correct repair; retuning existing salience may restore the object more cleanly.

## Material translation

Known chemicals may act as narrow recognizers, broad family cues, modifiers,
or structural carriers. EOs and absolutes may contribute a richly configural
natural object whose lot variation and matrix behavior remain uncertain. The
natural's constituent model can explain OAV or analytical risk, but formula
identity remains attached to the actual bottle and lot, not an imagined
reconstruction.

For florals, petal, flesh, pollen, nectar, stem, wax, humidity, fruit, spice,
tea, or animalic cues are selected only when they serve the named flower. For
woods, species-like grain, cream, bark, root, resin, polish, smoke, mineral, and
temperature can define the object. For leather, grain, suede nap, tannin, oil,
smoke, animal warmth, and wear perform analogous identity work. Every family
has its own anatomy; using the same generic wood, musk, sweetness, and radiance
chassis beneath different names creates nominal variety, not object variety.

Materials can also be **identity-neutral supports**. A carrier, fixative, or
diffusion aid may help delivery without becoming part of the intended object.
That role is legitimate but must not be counted as recognizable depth unless a
controlled sensory change demonstrates it.

## Interactions

Identity arises through relations among cues, so omission can have indirect
effects. Removing a bridge may not delete a note; it may split a coherent
flower and wood into two unrelated planes. Removing shadow may make the target
brighter but less recognizable because relief disappears. Reducing one strong
cue can reveal suppressed anatomy. These outcomes cannot be predicted from
isolated descriptors alone.

Expertise and expectation modify extraction. Training can encourage elemental
analysis, while synthetic processing favors the whole. A name or reference can
also prime category expectations. For this reason, identity testing should
include both target-aware diagnostic work and blinded recognition or matching
tasks. Target-aware evaluation explains why a design failed; blinded work
reduces demand characteristics. Neither alone provides the complete answer.

## Failure modes

**Genericization** occurs when the design retains family membership but loses
the named object. **Category substitution** occurs when a neighboring object
takes over: champi becomes jasmine, citrus becomes cologne, suede becomes
powdered musk, tobacco becomes vanilla amber. **Hierarchy inversion** occurs
when support becomes lead. **Split-plane failure** occurs when modules are
individually recognizable but do not form one perfume. **Reference capture**
occurs when a benchmark's obvious features replace the new target.

Other failures include natural-material essentialism (“it contains the
flower, therefore it is realistic”), descriptor literalism, recognizer
overdosing, and identity claims made only at opening. A perfume that displays
the target for thirty seconds and then becomes an unrelated base has not
demonstrated persistent identity merely because it lasts.

## Observables

Predeclare identity endpoints: free naming, forced-choice category, target
typicality, recognizer presence, lead/co-lead hierarchy, realism subtype,
forbidden drift, and confidence. Record them at multiple time windows. Separate
“I recognize the target,” “the target feels realistic,” “the target is clear,”
and “I like it.” These are not redundant questions.

For bouquets and hybrids, record both whole-object coherence and subject
retention. An assessor can rate a bouquet coherent while one named flower is
lost; that may be acceptable or a failure depending on the target. Use
assessor-level results, not only means. A minority with specific anosmia may
experience a different hierarchy.

## Controlled tests

The primary test is a carrier-matched anchor omission. Freeze the formula,
remove the declared anchor or recognizer module, replace raw volume with the
same carrier, and compare blinded at fixed application mass. If identity does
not change repeatably, the claimed anchor role is unsupported or the protocol
is insensitive.

Use ratio sweeps when the object depends on balance: low, target, and high
levels around the current dose, ideally with constant total active mass where
practical. Use substitution tests to distinguish an exact recognizer from a
generic family cue. Use bridge ablation to detect split planes. Use temporal
recognizer tests at opening, five minutes, thirty minutes, two hours, four
hours, and later windows appropriate to the target. Include repeated coded
controls and balanced order.

For realism, compare anatomy-specific endpoints rather than asking one vague
question. A flower may gain petal realism and lose living stem context; a wood
may gain cream and lose grain. A total realism vote conceals such tradeoffs.

## Claim boundary

The presence of recognizer materials, an accepted architecture contract, or a
modeled OAV pattern does not establish identity or realism. Formula names and
benchmark labels are hypotheses. Similar ingredients do not prove sensory
similarity. All sensory identity, realism, and similarity outcomes remain
`NOT TESTED` until exact-formula physical observations are bound to a declared
protocol.

## Primary literature

- Jinks AG, Laing DG. *The analysis of odor mixtures by humans: evidence for a
  configurational process.* Physiology & Behavior 72 (2001), 51–63.
  https://doi.org/10.1016/S0031-9384(00)00407-8
- Livermore A, Laing DG. *The influence of chemical complexity on the
  perception of multicomponent odor mixtures.* Perception & Psychophysics 60
  (1998), 650–661. https://doi.org/10.3758/BF03206052
- Le Berre E et al. *Just noticeable differences in component concentrations
  modify the odor quality of a blending mixture.* Chemical Senses 33 (2008),
  389–395. https://doi.org/10.1093/chemse/bjn006
- Barkat S et al. *Perceptual blending in odor mixtures depends on the nature
  of odorants and human olfactory expertise.* Chemical Senses 37 (2012),
  159–166. https://doi.org/10.1093/chemse/bjr086
- Le Berre E et al. *Perceptual processing strategy and exposure influence the
  perception of odor mixtures.* Chemical Senses 33 (2008), 193–199.
  https://doi.org/10.1093/chemse/bjm080

